---
description: Bind variable-length SQL parameter lists as an array, not a dynamically-sized IN (?,?,...)
alwaysApply: true
---

# Variable-length SQL parameter lists

When a query needs to filter by a set of values whose size varies per call
(e.g. `WHERE col IN (...)` over a batch of names/ids), do not build the
placeholder list by joining one `?` per value. The resulting SQL text is a
different literal string every time the size changes, which defeats
prepared-statement plan caching (the database re-parses and re-plans a new
query shape on every call) and can approach parameter-count limits at scale.

Prefer a single array bind instead:

- **PostgreSQL/JDBC**: `WHERE col = ANY(?)`, binding one `java.sql.Array`
  (`connection.createArrayOf(...)`) for the whole collection — one stable,
  cacheable query text regardless of how many values are in the collection.

Only fall back to a dynamically-sized `IN (?,?,...)` list when the target
database genuinely cannot bind an array for the driver in use (for example, a
test double or a database without array support) — and note that constraint
at the call site when you do.
